WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.680
Why is counter increment unsafe

2
00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:04.960
when processes share it? A

3
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.300
statement that looks like one

4
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.920
operation in source code can

5
00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.740
require several machine-level steps. If

6
00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:12.840
another execution accesses the same

7
00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:14.780
storage between those steps, the

8
00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:16.420
final value can depend on

9
00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:17.280
the interleaving.

10
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:19.320
Start with shared counter five,

11
00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:21.380
a producer that increments once

12
00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:23.200
and a consumer that decrements

13
00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:25.620
once. Serial execution in either

14
00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.020
order should return the counter

15
00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:28.700
to five. That gives us

16
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:31.060
an independently expected result before

17
00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:32.500
inspecting the faulty trace.

18
00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:34.600
An increment can load the

19
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.720
counter into a register, add

20
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.220
one to the register, then

21
00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.200
store the register back. A

22
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.740
decrement has its own load,

23
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.280
arithmetic and store. The registers

24
00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.620
belong to the executing contexts;

25
00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:47.900
the counter is the shared

26
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:48.760
memory location.

27
00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:51.400
The producer loads five and

28
00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:52.980
computes six, but has not

29
00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.200
yet stored it. A context

30
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.400
switch preserves that register value

31
00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.580
while the consumer runs. The

32
00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.440
shared counter still holds five,

33
00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.260
so the consumer can load

34
00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:04.940
the old value too.

35
00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.320
The consumer subtracts one and

36
00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.740
obtains four in its own

37
00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:11.420
register. Both arithmetic results now

38
00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:13.720
exist privately: six and four.

39
00:01:14.300 --> 00:01:16.420
Neither result represents both updates

40
00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:17.420
applied in sequence.

41
00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:20.260
The producer resumes and stores

42
00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:21.860
six into the shared counter.

43
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.080
Then the consumer resumes and

44
00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.000
stores four, overwriting that first

45
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.160
result. The final counter is

46
00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:29.480
four rather than the expected

47
00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.600
five, which is the lost-update

48
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:32.940
problem shown in the lecture.

49
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.120
Reverse the final two stores

50
00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:36.720
and the result can instead

51
00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.060
be six. That different outcome

52
00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:40.660
is not a different arithmetic

53
00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.600
rule; it is another legal

54
00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.740
unsynchronized interleaving. A race concerns

55
00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:48.340
conflicting shared access, including at

56
00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:49.500
least one modification.

57
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.500
Mutual exclusion can protect the

58
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.580
whole update sequence so one

59
00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:56.800
process completes its read, modify

60
00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:58.280
and write before the other

61
00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:01.120
enters. Merely preserving each process

62
00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.700
registers during a switch does

63
00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.180
not provide this exclusion. Correct

64
00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.320
context switching can coexist with

65
00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:09.280
an incorrect shared-data protocol.

66
00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:12.540
Two processes with only separate

67
00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:14.400
private counters do not share

68
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:17.100
this same memory location. Threads

69
00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:19.060
in one address space, explicit

70
00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:21.080
shared memory, or shared kernel

71
00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:22.580
data can create the shared

72
00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:25.560
access instead. Identify the actual

73
00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:27.400
storage and access path before

74
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.720
labeling every concurrent example a

75
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:29.960
race.

76
00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:31.720
Things to take note of.

77
00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:33.820
Expand source statements into reads,

78
00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.120
arithmetic and writes, track private

79
00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.020
registers separately, and name the

80
00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:39.740
interleaving that loses an update.

81
00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:41.720
The expected answer follows from

82
00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:43.660
the required combined effect, not

83
00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:45.260
from whichever final store happens

84
00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:45.960
to run last.
